<figure class="purchase-figure wide" aria-label="One purchase includes its retained result and acknowledgement">
  <figcaption><span>Figure 1</span><strong>The contract that must cross the boundary</strong></figcaption>
  <div class="purchase-boundary">
    <p class="purchase-label">One committed transaction · customer 1 · purchase · <code>purchase-103</code></p>
    <div class="purchase-grid">
      <div><strong>Guarded stock</strong><p>P3: 12 → 10<br/>P7: 1 → 0<br/>P9: 4 → 4</p></div>
      <div><strong>Agreed purchase</strong><p>103 · pending<br/>P7 × 1 at 20.00<br/>P3 × 2 at 4.50<br/><b>29.00 · no payment</b></p></div>
      <div><strong>Retained request</strong><p>Same canonical basket<br/><code>purchase-103 → 103</code><br/>Repeat returns this result</p></div>
      <div><strong>Durable work</strong><p><code>order-103-ack</code><br/>Pending order received<br/>29.00 · ready to dispatch</p></div>
    </div>
    <p class="purchase-note">All four parts commit together. Dispatch happens later, outside the purchase.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="purchase-caption">An independent replay from before order 103. The purchase consumes stock once, records an agreement and retains two different identities: the caller’s purchase and the acknowledgement work. Copying only orders and lines loses part of that contract.</p>
</figure>
